  "markscheme": {
    "html": "<p>Correct answer: D</p>\n<p>A: This configuration represents a couple. While the net force is zero (<span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"F - F = 0\"></span>), there is a non-zero net torque, causing the bar to rotate.</p>\n<p>B: In this case, the net force is not zero (<span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"F + F - F = F\"></span> upward), so the bar will undergo translational acceleration.</p>\n<p>C: Although the net force is zero (<span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"F + F - 2F = 0\"></span>), the downward force is not centered. This creates a net clockwise torque about the center of the bar, so it is not in rotational equilibrium.</p>\n<p>D: Static equilibrium requires both the net force and the net torque to be zero.<br/>- Net Force: <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"F + F - 2F = 0\"></span><br/>- Net Torque: Taking moments about the center, <span class=\"math\" data-latex=\"(F \\times d) - (F \\times d) + (2F \\times 0) = 0\"></span>.<br/>Both conditions are satisfied.</p>"
  },
  "paper": "paper_1a",
  "question": {
    "html": "<p>A uniform rigid bar is subjected to multiple vertical forces acting within the same vertical plane. The weight of the bar is negligible compared to the applied forces.</p>\n<p>In which of the following configurations is the bar in static equilibrium?</p><ol class=\"mcq-options\"><li data-label=\"A\"><p><figure data-asset-id=\"fig_01\"><img alt=\"Source question diagram 1\" src=\"asset:fig_01\"/></figure></p></li><li data-label=\"B\"><p><figure data-asset-id=\"fig_02\"><img alt=\"Source question diagram 2\" src=\"asset:fig_02\"/></figure></p></li><li data-label=\"C\"><p><figure data-asset-id=\"fig_03\"><img alt=\"Source question diagram 3\" src=\"asset:fig_03\"/></figure></p></li><li data-label=\"D\"><p><figure data-asset-id=\"fig_04\"><img alt=\"Source question diagram 4\" src=\"asset:fig_04\"/></figure></p></li></ol>"
  },
